import{_ as t,o,c as a,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.BJFNwp4n.js";const s="/images/open-source/kafka/message-flow.en.svg",g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Kafka Concepts and When to Use It","description":"Kafka concepts and when to use it: the full path of one record, cluster and partition concepts, replica roles, log storage, the three acks levels, core capabilities, typical applications, performance and comparison with similar middleware.","frontmatter":{"description":"Kafka concepts and when to use it: the full path of one record, cluster and partition concepts, replica roles, log storage, the three acks levels, core capabilities, typical applications, performance and comparison with similar middleware.","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","recommend":2,"tag":["Kafka"],"tags":["Kafka","Docker","KRaft"],"categories":["Open Source"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/open-source/kafka/introduction.md","filePath":"en/open-source/kafka/introduction.md"}'),n={name:"en/open-source/kafka/introduction.md"};function i(d,e,c,l,h,p){return o(),a("div",{"data-pagefind-body":!0,"data-pagefind-meta":"date:1794441600000"},[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="kafka-concepts-and-when-to-use-it" tabindex="-1">Kafka Concepts and When to Use It <a class="header-anchor" href="#kafka-concepts-and-when-to-use-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kafka Concepts and When to Use It&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Kafka is a distributed commit log: records are only appended to the end of a log on disk, and many consumers read the same data at their own pace. It targets high-throughput event streams rather than per-message delivery queues that delete records once read.</p><p>One record travels the whole path: the Producer picks a partition and writes to that partition&#39;s Leader replica, Followers keep it in sync as ISR, and a consumer belonging to some Consumer Group later pulls it by offset. Demo code and startup scripts live at <a href="https://github.com/zq-xu/zq-xu.github.io/tree/master/docs/public/demos/kafka" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>docs/public/demos/kafka</code></a>.</p><h2 id="what-kafka-is" tabindex="-1">What Kafka is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-kafka-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Kafka is&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In short: <strong>Kafka is a distributed commit log that appends records to disk in order, and lets many consumers read at their own pace.</strong></p><p>Three keywords carry the idea:</p><ul><li><strong>Sequential writes</strong>: records are only appended to the end of a log, and sequential disk I/O is far faster than random writes;</li><li><strong>Not deleted on delivery</strong>: records expire by a retention policy (time or size), not because someone read them, so the same data can be replayed;</li><li><strong>Independent progress</strong>: every consumer keeps its own offset, so slow and fast consumers do not affect each other.</li></ul><p>This is what makes it a <strong>high-throughput event streaming platform</strong> rather than a task queue.</p><h2 id="the-full-path-of-one-record" tabindex="-1">The full path of one record <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-full-path-of-one-record" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The full path of one record&quot;">​</a></h2><img class="kafka-flow" src="'+s+'" alt="The full path of a record: producer -&gt; topic / partitions inside the broker -&gt; consumer group, with offsets committed to __consumer_offsets"><p>The diagram splits Kafka into three layers: <strong>clients (producer / consumer)</strong>, <strong>storage inside the broker (topic / partition / replica)</strong>, and <strong>offset storage (<code>__consumer_offsets</code>)</strong>. The numbered steps ①-③ mark the life of one record; the list below follows that path.</p><blockquote><p>The diagram shows a single broker for brevity. In reality each partition&#39;s replicas are spread across brokers; &quot;leader&quot; here means the leader of that partition, not a property of a broker. The mechanism section below spells this out.</p></blockquote><ol><li><strong>The producer picks a partition</strong>: with a key it lands on <code>hash(key) % partitions</code>; without a key it round-robins or sticks to one partition. This choice decides which records share a queue.</li><li><strong>The write goes to the partition leader</strong>: the producer only writes to the leader replica; the broker appends the record to the end of that partition&#39;s log and returns its offset. With <code>acks=all</code> it also waits for the whole ISR (in-sync replicas) to persist the record (see &quot;Write acknowledgement: the three <code>acks</code> levels&quot; below).</li><li><strong>Followers catch up, forming the ISR</strong>: followers continuously fetch from the leader; replicas that keep up stay in the ISR, and a new leader is elected from it if the current one fails.</li><li><strong>The record gets an offset inside the partition</strong>: the offset is a line number that increases strictly within one partition; there is no order across partitions.</li><li><strong>Consumers fetch by offset</strong>: a consumer belongs to a consumer group, and the group assigns partitions to members — <strong>one partition belongs to exactly one member at a time</strong>. Different groups are independent.</li><li><strong>The offset is committed</strong>: after processing, &quot;the next offset to read&quot; is written to <code>__consumer_offsets</code>. Offsets live apart from the records, so rewinding an offset replays history.</li><li><strong>Retention deletes old data</strong>: expired log segments (size- or time-based chunks of the partition log) are removed, but offsets are not reset; reading from an already deleted offset yields an out-of-range error.</li></ol><blockquote><p>Only step 2 writes; every other step reads a different position of the same log. That is the essential difference between Kafka and a queue that deletes on delivery.</p></blockquote><h2 id="concept-overview" tabindex="-1">Concept overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#concept-overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Concept overview&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kafka&#39;s concepts are not a flat list but a containment chain from cluster to partition. The next three subsections cover storage, producers and consumers; replica roles, the on-disk log and write acknowledgement are covered under &quot;Replicas, log storage and write acknowledgement&quot;.</p><h3 id="cluster-and-storage" tabindex="-1">Cluster and storage <a class="header-anchor" href="#cluster-and-storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cluster and storage&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The containment chain from largest to smallest is:</p><p><strong>Cluster → Broker (one of them also acts as controller) → Topic → Partition → Replica</strong>; an <strong>offset</strong> is a position inside a partition, a <strong>consumer group</strong> is the unit of assignment on the read side, and <strong><code>__consumer_offsets</code></strong> stores each group&#39;s progress.</p><p>At the cluster level there are two roles: a <strong>broker</strong> stores data; a <strong>controller</strong> maintains metadata (which topics exist, which broker holds each partition&#39;s replicas, who leads which partition) and decides partition leader elections.</p><p>Early Kafka kept that metadata in an external ZooKeeper ensemble. From 3.x it uses built-in <strong>KRaft (Kafka Raft)</strong> instead: Raft consensus elects a controller inside Kafka, which manages metadata and replica assignment with no ZooKeeper. The single-node cluster in this guide runs in KRaft mode, so its one broker also acts as the controller.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Level</th><th>Definition</th><th>How it relates to the others</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Broker</td><td>Cluster</td><td>One Kafka server node</td><td>A cluster has several brokers; each holds replicas of some partitions</td></tr><tr><td>Controller</td><td>Cluster</td><td>Owns metadata, partition assignment and leader election; runs on one of the brokers</td><td><strong>Not a partition leader</strong>; one active controller per cluster</td></tr><tr><td>Topic</td><td>Topic</td><td>A logical category of records, like a table name</td><td>Contains N partitions fixed at creation; a topic itself has no leader</td></tr><tr><td>Partition</td><td>Partition</td><td>A slice of a topic, one append-only log</td><td>The unit of ordering and parallelism; each partition has several replicas</td></tr><tr><td>Replica</td><td>Partition</td><td>One copy of a specific partition, placed on a broker</td><td>Replica count comes from <code>replication.factor</code> (a topic setting, but <strong>each partition owns its own replica set</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td>Offset</td><td>Partition</td><td>A strictly increasing number inside one partition</td><td>Only meaningful within that partition; committed positions are offsets too</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="producer" tabindex="-1">Producer <a class="header-anchor" href="#producer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Producer&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The producer is the write client: it chooses a partition, batches, compresses, retries and can be idempotent. It sends records only to the leader of the target partition; the leader/follower split is covered under key mechanisms.</p><ul><li><strong>Partition choice</strong>: with a key it computes <code>hash(key) % partitions</code>; without a key it round-robins or sticks to one partition, keeping a batch together where possible.</li><li><strong>Batching and compression</strong>: <code>batch.size</code> and <code>linger.ms</code> control batch size and waiting time; compression happens in the client (<code>compression.type</code>) and the broker stores the batch as-is.</li><li><strong>Write acknowledgement</strong>: <code>acks</code> decides how many replicas must confirm before the send returns; see the comparison below.</li><li><strong>Retries and idempotence</strong>: retries can create duplicates; with idempotence enabled (<code>enable.idempotence=true</code>), the broker deduplicates by producer ID and sequence number.</li></ul><h3 id="consumers-and-consumer-groups" tabindex="-1">Consumers and consumer groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#consumers-and-consumer-groups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consumers and consumer groups&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The consumer is the read client: it fetches records by offset and processes them. Several consumers can form a consumer group that subscribes to the same topic.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Definition</th><th>How it relates to the others</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Consumer</td><td>Read client: fetch by offset and process</td><td>Must belong to a consumer group; commits offsets manually or automatically</td></tr><tr><td>Consumer group</td><td>A set of consumers sharing one subscription</td><td>Partitions are split exclusively inside the group; groups are independent of each other</td></tr><tr><td>Group coordinator</td><td>Broker-side component managing groups and rebalance</td><td>A membership change triggers a rebalance, during which consumption pauses</td></tr><tr><td>Rebalance</td><td>Reassignment of partitions inside a group</td><td>Happens when members join/leave or the subscription changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>__consumer_offsets</code></td><td>Internal topic holding committed offsets</td><td>Stores &quot;the next offset to read&quot; per (group, topic, partition)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Inside one group a partition belongs to exactly one member at a time; different groups keep their own offsets, so the same log can be read by several groups at different paces.</p><h3 id="boundaries-that-are-easy-to-confuse" tabindex="-1">Boundaries that are easy to confuse <a class="header-anchor" href="#boundaries-that-are-easy-to-confuse" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Boundaries that are easy to confuse&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Ordering boundary</strong>: order exists only inside a partition; only the same key is guaranteed to land on the same partition.</li><li><strong>Parallelism boundary</strong>: one group can use at most as many consumers as there are partitions; extra consumers just idle (useful as hot standbys).</li><li><strong>Acknowledgement boundary</strong>: Kafka acknowledges an offset, not an individual record; duplicates are handled with idempotent production or transactions.</li><li><strong>Partition-count boundary</strong>: partitions can only be added, never removed; expanding changes the key-to-partition mapping, and existing records are not redistributed.</li></ul><h2 id="replicas-log-storage-and-write-acknowledgement" tabindex="-1">Replicas, log storage and write acknowledgement <a class="header-anchor" href="#replicas-log-storage-and-write-acknowledgement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Replicas, log storage and write acknowledgement&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="leader-and-follower-replica-roles-of-a-partition" tabindex="-1">Leader and follower: replica roles of a partition <a class="header-anchor" href="#leader-and-follower-replica-roles-of-a-partition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Leader and follower: replica roles of a partition&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In short: <strong>leader and follower are replica roles of each partition; they belong neither to the cluster as a whole nor to the topic.</strong></p><ul><li>A topic is only a collection of partitions and has no leader itself; <code>replication.factor</code> is set on the topic but applies to each of its partitions.</li><li>The replicas of a partition are spread across brokers; one becomes its leader and the others followers. <strong>Every partition elects its own leader independently.</strong></li><li>So one broker is often the leader of some partitions and the follower of others at the same time.</li><li>The replicas of that partition that keep up with its leader form the <strong>ISR (In-Sync Replicas)</strong>. If the leader fails, a new leader is elected from the ISR; <code>acks=all</code> waits for the ISR to confirm.</li><li>At the cluster level there is also a controller, which owns metadata and leader election; it is a different concept from a partition leader.</li></ul><p>Take three brokers, one topic with three partitions and <code>replication.factor=3</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Partition</th><th>Leader</th><th>Followers</th><th>ISR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P0</td><td>Broker 1</td><td>Broker 2, Broker 3</td><td>Broker 1, 2, 3</td></tr><tr><td>P1</td><td>Broker 2</td><td>Broker 1, Broker 3</td><td>Broker 1, 2, 3</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Broker 3</td><td>Broker 1, Broker 2</td><td>Broker 1, 2, 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When writing P0, the producer connects to Broker 1 only; Broker 2 and 3 follow it. Broker 1 is also a follower of P1 and P2, which does not affect its read/write role for P0.</p><h3 id="log-storage-segments-retention-and-compaction" tabindex="-1">Log storage: segments, retention and compaction <a class="header-anchor" href="#log-storage-segments-retention-and-compaction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Log storage: segments, retention and compaction&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A partition is a directory on disk, and its log is not one ever-growing file but a series of <strong>segments</strong>:</p><ul><li>new records append only to the current active segment (the one being written);</li><li>when it reaches <code>segment.bytes</code> (1GB by default) or <code>segment.ms</code> (7 days), it is sealed and a new segment starts;</li><li>deletion works on <strong>whole segments</strong>. When <code>retention.ms</code> / <code>retention.bytes</code> is exceeded, old segments are dropped entirely; Kafka never deletes a few records inside a segment.</li></ul><p>So retention means &quot;old data disappears&quot;, not &quot;offsets reset&quot;: offsets are logical positions and never go backwards; reading an offset that has been deleted returns an out-of-range error.</p><p>How old segments are handled is decided by the topic-level <code>cleanup.policy</code>, which has two values:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>delete</code> (default)</td><td>Drop whole old segments by time or size</td><td>Ordinary event streams, log collection</td></tr><tr><td><code>compact</code></td><td>Do not drop by age; keep only the latest record per key</td><td>State snapshots, changelogs, config replication</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>compact</code> is not a compression codec such as gzip or snappy; it folds history by key. Suppose the same key is written three times:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Key</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><code>user-1</code></td><td><code>v1</code></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>user-1</code></td><td><code>v2</code></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>user-1</code></td><td><code>v3</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Once compaction has run, each key ends up with a single record: in the example only offset 9 with <code>v3</code> remains; offsets 0 and 5 are cleaned up, but offset 9 stays where it was, so the log has offset gaps. To delete a key, write a record whose value is <code>null</code> (a tombstone); after <code>delete.retention.ms</code> the tombstone is removed as well.</p><p>That also explains the point of compaction: one latest state per key, so a consumer replaying from the beginning can rebuild the current state. It suits changelogs and state-like topics.</p><h3 id="write-acknowledgement-the-three-acks-levels" tabindex="-1">Write acknowledgement: the three <code>acks</code> levels <a class="header-anchor" href="#write-acknowledgement-the-three-acks-levels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Write acknowledgement: the three `acks` levels&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>acks</code> is a <strong>producer setting</strong> that decides who must confirm a record before the send counts as successful.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Who confirms</th><th>Cost and risk</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>No one; the send succeeds immediately</td><td>Lowest latency, highest throughput; loss is possible and invisible to the producer if a broker never receives the record or the connection breaks</td><td>Logs and metrics where loss is acceptable</td></tr><tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The leader, after writing to its local log</td><td>Medium latency; if the leader dies before followers fetch it, the new leader may lack the record</td><td>Ordinary business data tolerant of rare loss</td></tr><tr><td><code>all</code> (also <code>-1</code>)</td><td>Every replica in the ISR</td><td>Highest latency, lowest throughput; acknowledged records survive as long as the ISR is large enough</td><td>Orders, payments, state changes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Common points of confusion:</p><ul><li><code>acks</code> lives on the producer; <code>min.insync.replicas</code> lives on the broker/topic. Together they define the minimum replica count. With <code>acks=all</code> and <code>min.insync.replicas=2</code>, a write fails with <code>NotEnoughReplicas</code> when the ISR is smaller than 2 instead of quietly writing only the leader.</li><li>Kafka 3.x producers default to <code>acks=all</code>; choosing <code>0</code> or <code>1</code> explicitly trades reliability for throughput.</li><li>Idempotent production requires <code>acks=all</code>; with <code>acks=0/1</code>, retries can create duplicates.</li><li><code>acks</code> only covers the write to replicas. It says nothing about exactly-once consumption; end-to-end exactly-once needs transactions or idempotent consumers.</li></ul><h2 id="core-capabilities" tabindex="-1">Core capabilities <a class="header-anchor" href="#core-capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Core capabilities&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Read, write and durability</strong>: publish/subscribe, replay by <code>retention.ms</code>, the compaction cleanup policy (keep the latest record per key)</li><li><strong>Parallelism and fault tolerance</strong>: partition-level parallelism, replicas and ISR, consumer groups with automatic rebalance</li><li><strong>Ordering and reliability</strong>: per-partition ordering, idempotent production, transactions</li><li><strong>Ecosystem and governance</strong>: compression (gzip / snappy / lz4 / zstd), Kafka Connect, Kafka Streams, quotas and multi-tenancy</li></ul><h2 id="typical-applications" tabindex="-1">Typical applications <a class="header-anchor" href="#typical-applications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Typical applications&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Log and metric collection</li><li>User behaviour tracking</li><li>CDC (change data capture): binlog → Kafka → cache / search / warehouse</li><li>Event-driven microservices</li><li>Stream processing ingress (Flink / Kafka Streams)</li><li>Traffic smoothing and historical replay</li><li>Event sourcing and audit</li></ul><h2 id="performance-order-of-magnitude" tabindex="-1">Performance order of magnitude <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-order-of-magnitude" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance order of magnitude&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Order of magnitude</th><th>Factors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single broker writes</td><td>Hundreds of thousands to millions of small records/s, hundreds of MB/s</td><td>Record size, batching, compression, <code>acks</code>, sequential disk writes</td></tr><tr><td>Single broker reads</td><td>Same order or higher</td><td>Page cache, fetch size, decompression</td></tr><tr><td>Latency</td><td><code>acks=0</code> sub-millisecond; <code>acks=1</code> 1–3 ms; <code>acks=all</code> a few ms in one data center (DC)</td><td>acks, replica count, cross-DC RTT (round-trip time)</td></tr><tr><td>Single partition</td><td>Commonly around 10 MB/s</td><td>One partition is a sequential bottleneck; scale with partitions</td></tr><tr><td>Retention</td><td>TB to PB</td><td>Disk capacity, retention policy, compression ratio</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>MB/s, GB, TB and PB in this table are throughput and capacity units (1 PB = 1024 TB).</p></blockquote><p>Public reference points: LinkedIn has reported trillions of messages per day, and Netflix and Uber operate at millions of events per second.</p><p>Measured locally (single Docker broker, single Go client, small records, <code>acks=all</code> + idempotence):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sync produce (one ACK — acknowledgement — per record)</td><td>9–12 records in 19–29 ms ≈ <strong>400 records/s</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Async produce (batched)</td><td>200 records in 15 ms ≈ <strong>13,000 records/s</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 30× gap is entirely &quot;waiting for one network round trip per record&quot;. When throughput is low, first check whether the client is sending synchronously, one at a time.</p><h2 id="choosing-kafka" tabindex="-1">Choosing Kafka <a class="header-anchor" href="#choosing-kafka" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choosing Kafka&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Good fit</strong>: high-throughput event streams that must be durable and replayable; several downstream consumers reading the same data at different speeds; per-key ordering; a stream processing ingress or CDC; eventual consistency and asynchronous processing are acceptable.</p><p><strong>Poor fit</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Better choice</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complex routing, delayed queues, priorities</td><td>RabbitMQ</td><td>Kafka has no per-message routing or priority</td></tr><tr><td>Lightweight single-node &quot;just a queue&quot;</td><td>Redis Stream / RabbitMQ</td><td>Kafka has more moving parts and tuning</td></tr><tr><td>Task dispatch (per-message ack and retry)</td><td>RabbitMQ / SQS (Amazon Simple Queue Service)</td><td>Kafka acknowledges at partition-offset granularity</td></tr><tr><td>Huge multi-tenant setups with tiered storage</td><td>Pulsar</td><td>Kafka needs extra components</td></tr><tr><td>Synchronous service calls</td><td>gRPC / HTTP</td><td>A broker only adds latency</td></tr><tr><td>Tiny volumes (tens of thousands per day)</td><td>A database table / cron job</td><td>Operational cost outweighs the benefit</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In short: <strong>log/event streams + throughput + replay → Kafka; task distribution + fine routing + low-latency acknowledgement → RabbitMQ-style brokers.</strong></p><p>The local practice in this series uses the official Kafka 3.9.1 image <code>apache/kafka:3.9.1</code> in KRaft mode; creating and verifying that cluster is covered in <a href="./local-deployment.html">Running Kafka Locally</a>.</p><h2 id="versions-and-references" tabindex="-1">Versions and references <a class="header-anchor" href="#versions-and-references" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Versions and references&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Versions used in this series: Kafka <code>3.9.1</code> (KRaft mode), Go client sarama <code>v1.60.2</code>.</li><li><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apache Kafka documentation</a>: the authoritative source for configuration, protocol and operations.</li><li><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#kraft" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">KRaft mode</a>: metadata management without ZooKeeper.</li><li><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#configuration" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Kafka configuration</a>: the full list of broker, topic and client settings.</li></ul>',72)])])}const f=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,f as default};
